# Tests and goldens

- `test/` mirrors `lib/`. Unit tests for logic, widget tests for screens (loading, empty, error, offline, success).
- Golden tests cover Design System components only (no full-screen goldens).
- Full matrix for `AppButton`, `AppTextField`, `AppTabs`, `AppListItem`, `AppSheet`, `AppSwitch`:
  Light/Dark × en/fa(RTL) × text scale 1.0/2.0 × Compact/Medium, plus the test skin.
  Other components: Light and Dark × en × 1.0, plus one RTL case and one2.0× case.
- Goldens render from the same Gallery entries the Inspector uses. Fonts must be loaded in tests (no fallback boxes).
- Golden files are updated only on purpose (`--update-goldens`) and the diff is reviewed in the PR.
- Run goldens on one pinned CI OS with the pinned Flutter version.

## D-019 / D-020 migration

Apply to packages/design_system/test and apps/design_system_viewer/test as well. The owner explicitly excludes creating new golden baselines for the D-019/D-020 migration; use real-font geometry regression tests without claiming visual approval.

## D-044 / D-047 readiness policy

D-047 authorizes initial DS-only Windows baselines, all pending owner review. Golden tests carry the golden tag and use bundled real fonts, fixed surfaces/DPR1 and disabled animations. Seven sheets per family: fa-light1.0,fa-dark1.0,en-light1.0,en-dark1.0,fa-dark2.0,en-light2.0 with product skin, plusen-light1.0 test skin. Families: Button,Badge,TextField,Password,OTP,Radio,AppIcon,App Bar,Navigator,PageScaffold. PageScaffold runs each sheet at402 and700. Include every source-axis value and loading; omit/list unavailable sources, never fabricate. No tolerant comparator. Verify twice without updating; non-determinism is a stop condition. Linux CI excludes golden because Windows owns reference pixels. D-044 supersedes the previous1.3 content matrix; only App Bar text and Navigator item labels cap locally at 1.3, Inspector comparison remains 1.0. Every future product screen is tested compact at1.0/2.0 × fa/en × Light/Dark, with scrolling content and no clipping.
